<body>
  <header>
    <div class="shell">
      <nav><a class="logo" href="#top">Ez<span>shopper</span></a><div><a href="#experience">Experience</a><a href="#models">Models</a><a href="#launch">Launch</a></div></nav>
      <div id="top" class="hero-grid">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Concept brief · September 2026</p><h1>Shopping, simplified.</h1><p class="lede">Ezshopper helps people describe what they need, compare the few options that fit, and complete a multi-store purchase from one trusted account.</p></div>
        <div class="signal"><strong>One decision.</strong><span class="muted">The opportunity is bigger than search: give shoppers confidence that they chose well, then make the purchase easy.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="shell">
    <section id="experience"><p class="section-label">01 · The consumer problem</p><h2>Turn an open-ended search into a guided decision.</h2><p class="intro">“Jogging sweatpants” returns products from many stores with different brands, discounts, fabrics, fit, shipping, and returns. A larger grid can preserve the same mental burden. Ezshopper should ask only the questions that improve the decision.</p>
      <div class="quote">“I want jogging sweatpants under $60, for outdoor running, with zippered pockets.”</div>
      <div class="cards"><article class="card"><span class="number">1</span><h3>Best match</h3><p class="muted">Meets the shopper’s budget, use case, fabric, and feature requirements.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">2</span><h3>Lowest delivered price</h3><p class="muted">Shows the real total after shipping, eligible discounts, and tax estimates.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="number">3</span><h3>Best alternative</h3><p class="muted">Explains the one trade-off that makes a slightly different product worthwhile.</p></article></div>
      <div class="callout"><h3>Design principle</h3><p>Compare total delivered cost, available size, material, delivery estimate, return cost, and the difference between identical products and merely similar ones. Never imply durability or fit from an image alone.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section><p class="section-label">02 · Merchant relationships</p><h2>Product data is available—but permission matters.</h2><p class="intro">Ezshopper can work with affiliate programs, licensed product feeds, and direct merchant APIs. A data feed can authorize catalog display without authorizing Ezshopper to complete checkout on the merchant’s behalf.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>What it covers</th><th>What to negotiate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Catalog permission</strong></td><td>Images, descriptions, prices, variants, and availability.</td><td>Trademark use, caching, refresh frequency, attribution, and termination.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transaction permission</strong></td><td>Orders, payment, cancellations, returns, and customer support.</td><td>Order acceptance, promotions, data sharing, commissions, disputes, and service levels.</td></tr></tbody></table>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:18px">Authorized feeds or APIs are a better foundation than a business built around scraping retail pages. Some stores will welcome qualified incremental customers; others will resist losing the direct relationship. The pitch should be measurable: new buyers and an acquisition cost the merchant can justify.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="models"><p class="section-label">03 · One account, multiple stores</p><h2>One account, one basket, several underlying orders.</h2><p class="intro">“One account,” “one checkout,” and “one card charge” are separate promises. Ezshopper can coordinate five store orders while still showing five packages, five fulfillment timelines, and five return policies.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Who sells to the shopper?</th><th>Checkout</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Affiliate comparison</strong></td><td>Each store</td><td>Shopper checks out at each store</td><td>Fastest launch; leaves the checkout friction intact.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contracted marketplace</strong></td><td>Participating stores</td><td>Ezshopper coordinates checkout and payment</td><td>Best customer experience; requires merchant integrations.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Buying agent</strong></td><td>Usually the merchant, with Ezshopper acting under contract</td><td>Ezshopper places an authorized purchase</td><td>Requires careful merchant and payments classification.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reseller / dropshipper</strong></td><td>Ezshopper</td><td>Customer buys directly from Ezshopper</td><td>More control and responsibility, including inventory and returns exposure.</td></tr></tbody></table>
      <div class="flow"><div class="step"><b>1 · Save</b>Address and payment method once.</div><div class="step"><b>2 · Verify</b>Refresh price, stock, delivery, and tax.</div><div class="step"><b>3 · Approve</b>Customer confirms the basket and trade-offs.</div><div class="step"><b>4 · Allocate</b>Payment provider routes funds to merchants.</div><div class="step"><b>5 · Fulfill</b>Stores ship; Ezshopper owns the order dashboard.</div></div>
    </section>

    <section><p class="section-label">04 · Payments, tax, and compliance</p><h2>Tax follows the underlying transaction—not the number of bank transfers.</h2><p class="intro">A marketplace may collect one customer payment and remit tax on facilitated sales. A reseller buys for resale, then makes the retail sale. The exact result depends on state, product, delivery address, discounts, shipping, and the legal contracts.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Typical treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Marketplace</strong></td><td>Ezshopper may be the marketplace facilitator responsible for collecting and remitting sales tax. Participating sellers need documentation showing who collected.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reseller</strong></td><td>Ezshopper may purchase inventory for resale with the required documentation, then charge the customer tax on its retail sale.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ordinary retailer checkout</strong></td><td>If Ezshopper buys from a normal shopping site without a resale arrangement, that site may charge tax; a later customer sale can create a difficult tax and documentation problem.</td></tr></tbody></table>
      <div class="callout"><h3>Payments are a separate legal question</h3><p>Receiving a customer’s money and forwarding it to unrelated stores can raise money-transmission issues. A provider such as Stripe Connect can support marketplace payment flows, but participating merchants need connected accounts and the structure still needs state-by-state review. Use payment tokens; PCI DSS prohibits storing card verification codes after authorization.</p></div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:20px">State rules vary. For example, New York exempts qualifying clothing priced below $110 from state sales tax, while local treatment can differ. A national “one tax rate” assumption will fail.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="launch"><p class="section-label">05 · Practical launch path</p><h2>Start narrow enough to make the promise dependable.</h2><p class="intro">Begin with one category and a small set of willing merchants. Test whether a guided comparison and unified checkout create enough value to cover payment processing, support, fraud, returns, promotions, and customer acquisition.</p>
      <div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Measure decisions</h3><p class="muted">Time to a confident choice, completion rate, recommendation trust, and repeat use.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Measure operations</h3><p class="muted">Out-of-stock handling, price changes, split refunds, returns, and customer support contacts.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Measure economics</h3><p class="muted">Contribution after merchant commission, processing, acquisition, support, fraud, and returns.</p></article></div>
      <p class="quote">A $200 basket at a hypothetical 10% commission produces $20 before those costs. The product is promising only if the completed basket remains profitable.</p>
    </section>

    <section><p class="section-label">Further reading</p><h2>Sources behind the brief.</h2><div class="sources"><div class="source"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcps.2014.08.002">Choice overload meta-analysis</a><span class="muted">How complexity, uncertainty, and decision goals affect choice.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://affiliates.walmart.com/page/faqs">Walmart affiliate FAQ</a><span class="muted">Example of merchant product feeds and referral economics.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://www.tax.ny.gov/pubs_and_bulls/publications/sales/marketplace.htm">New York marketplace rules</a><span class="muted">Marketplace collection, registration, and seller documentation.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://www.cdtfa.ca.gov/formspubs/pub103/">California resale guidance</a><span class="muted">Resale certificates and purchases held for resale.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://docs.stripe.com/connect/separate-charges-and-transfers">Stripe Connect</a><span class="muted">Separate charges and transfers for connected merchants.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://www.pcisecuritystandards.org/faqs/1574/">PCI card-data guidance</a><span class="muted">Card verification codes cannot be retained after authorization.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://dfpi.ca.gov/rules-enforcement/laws-and-regulations/opinion-letters-by-law-subject/agent-of-payee/">California agent-of-payee guidance</a><span class="muted">A state example of payment-processing exemptions.</span></div><div class="source"><a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftcs-endorsement-guides-what-people-are-asking">FTC endorsement guidance</a><span class="muted">Clear disclosure for affiliate and sponsored recommendations.</span></div></div></section>
